---
// The developer quickstart at the top of /docs: the pitch on the left, real
// runnable code on the right.
//
// This is the one structural piece of the reference layout the documentation
// could not get any other way, and it exists because of what the page was.
// /docs opened with a heading and four paragraphs of markdown, which is the
// same shape as every other page on the site and therefore carried no more
// information than any of them. A reader landing on the documentation has one
// question — what does this look like in code — and the old page made them
// click through a section heading to find out.
//
// So the first thing on the page is the answer: a service that returns
// something real, next to the sentence saying what it does. The code is not
// an illustration. It is the file from the repository, and the version in the
// response is read from the release API at build time, so the snippet cannot
// claim a release that does not exist.
//
// Two columns, and they do not stack until there is no room for them. The
// asymmetry is deliberate and is the other half of why this reads as a
// document rather than a page: the prose column is narrower than the code
// column because the code is the thing being looked at, and a layout that
// gave them equal width would be saying they are equally important.
//
// It is one card rather than two cards side by side. A raised block with a
// seam down the middle reads as two things that happen to be adjacent; one
// block with a two-column interior reads as one thing with two parts, which
// is what it is.
//
// Two things about this component that are not style.
//
// `not-content` is not optional. src/styles/starlight.css styles prose links
// as `.sl-markdown-content :is(a):not(:where(.not-content *))`, which is
// specificity 0,1,1 and unlayered — and Tailwind's `text-paper` is 0,1,0 in
// `@layer utilities`, so the prose rule wins and the primary button renders
// its label in the button's own fill. It is invisible. That rule was written
// to style markdown links and had never met a site component, because until
// MDX landed no site component could be rendered inside the markdown content
// at all. `not-content` is Starlight's own escape hatch and it is the correct
// fix rather than loosening the selector: the prose rule is right about
// prose, and this is not prose.
//
// It stacks rather than splitting into columns, and that is a measurement
// rather than a preference.
//
// Starlight's content column is 44rem, so the card has at most 768px of
// usable width once the panel's padding is reclaimed. Split two ways with a
// 2.5rem gap that leaves roughly 270px of prose beside 370px of code. The
// code column then fits about forty monospace characters, and the shortest
// real line in this snippet is fifty. So a two-column version of this card
// cuts off the end of every line it shows — a code window whose whole job is
// to be read, showing half of itself. The reference splits because its
// content column is far wider than Starlight's; here the honest arrangement
// is the text above the code, with the code as wide as the card.
//
// The card is held to the content column rather than pushed out of it.
//
// It was reaching out with a negative margin to reclaim the panel's padding,
// which gained 64px and cost a horizontal scrollbar at 768px: the negative
// margin and the compensating `calc(100% + Nrem)` were each off by a
// half-rem against Starlight's `--sl-content-pad-x`, so the section ended up
// exactly 24px wider than the viewport. 64px of card is not worth a
// scrollbar that appears at a width readers actually use, and the code
// window measures as fitting at 704px either way.
import CodeBlock from "./CodeBlock.astro";
import LinkButton from "./LinkButton.astro";
import { LATEST } from "../lib/releases.mjs";

/*
  One route rather than two, and wrapped across lines.

  The home page shows both of health_service.orb's routes and has the full
  page width to do it. Here the widest line of the verbatim version measures
  892px against 640px of usable card, so it would scroll — and a hero whose
  first impression is a cut-off line teaches a reader the wrong thing about
  what the code does. Splitting the response across two bindings keeps every
  token the compiler sees and gets the widest line under 400px.

  It is also the better example. Building the body from two pieces is what
  the language actually looks like when a response carries more than one
  field, and it shows `+` on strings without needing a paragraph to explain
  it. The version is read from the release API at build time, so the snippet
  cannot claim a release that does not exist.
*/
const quickstart = `route GET "/health" {
    val uptime = system.uptime()
    val body = "{\\"status\\":\\"UP\\",\\"uptime_seconds\\":" + uptime
    return ok 200 body + ",\\"version\\":\\"${LATEST.version}\\"}"
}`;
---

<section
  class="not-content mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-6 pt-2 sm:px-8"
  aria-labelledby="quickstart-heading"
>
  <div
    class="rounded-[var(--radius-lg)] bg-[var(--surface)] p-6 shadow-[var(--shadow-sm)] sm:p-8"
  >
    <div class="max-w-[52ch]">
      <p class="label">Developer quickstart</p>
      <h2 id="quickstart-heading" class="text-title mt-4 text-balance font-display">
        A service is a list of routes.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--ink-2)]">
        Each route takes a request, does one small thing, and returns a status
        and a body. Orbit compiles it to C, your platform's compiler produces
        the executable, and the result ships as a single file with no runtime
        beside it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <LinkButton href="/docs/start/getting-started/" variant="primary">
          Getting started
        </LinkButton>
        <LinkButton href="/docs/start/tour/" variant="secondary" chevron>
          Language tour
        </LinkButton>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <CodeBlock code={quickstart} label="examples/health_service.orb" />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>